# Pin gwenesis's hot interpreter code into internal IRAM.
#
# Measured 2026-08-02: gwenesis is the only emulator core in this repo with
# zero bytes in IRAM0 .text (`python3 -m esp_idf_size --archives
# build/gwenesis.map`: libgwenesis.a shows "IRAM0 .text 0"). Every other core
# pins its hot loop -- libnofrendo.a 20240B, libsmsplus.a 14494B, libgnuboy.a
# 14596B, libsnes9x.a 41408B (all via IRAM_ATTR in their sources, e.g.
# retro-core/components/nofrendo/nes/cpu.c). gwenesis has none.
#
# The reason it matters more here: m68kcpu.c #includes m68kops.h (Musashi's
# generated opcode-handler table) into one translation unit. Split by section
# with `riscv32-esp-elf-size -A`, m68kcpu.c.obj alone is 247384B .text (the
# dispatch loop + ~2000 generated opcode handlers) and 308140B .rodata (the
# 65536-entry instruction jump table plus per-opcode cycle tables, read on
# every single M68K instruction fetch). The .rodata alone is more than double
# CONFIG_CACHE_L2_CACHE_SIZE (0x20000 = 128KB), so an opcode dispatch is close
# to a guaranteed L2 miss out to flash -- on every instruction, not
# occasionally. Compare retro-core's other cores: libsnes9x.a's whole flash
# .text is 280588B, still large, but SNES gets 27fps @ 96% BUSY (half speed)
# where gwenesis got 15.6fps historically and measures 3-4fps now -- and SNES
# has 41KB pinned where gwenesis has 0.
#
# Reference point: the STM32 game-and-watch-retro-go-sd port (weaker
# hardware: single 280MHz M7, no PSRAM) doesn't rely on caching this code at
# all -- it copies the *entire* Mega Drive core (interpreter, VDP, Z80,
# everything) into a ~800KB zero-wait AXI SRAM overlay region before running
# a single opcode (STM32H7B0VBTx_FLASH.ld: .overlay_md / .overlay_md_bss at
# __RAM_EMU_START__, loaded AT> CORES i.e. copied from flash to RAM at boot).
# Mega Drive is playable there. We don't have 800KB of headroom on this app
# partition (`esp_idf_size build/gwenesis.map` showed ~310KB free D/IRAM,
# 46.2% used, before this change) so we can't replicate that fully -- we pin
# what fits.
#
# Budget: the first attempt tried m68kcpu's .text (247384B) plus everything
# except Z80.c (~278KB total). That failed to link: `--enable-non-contiguous-
# regions discards section ... Total discarded sections size is 179593
# bytes`. The 46.2%-used / ~310KB-free figure from `esp_idf_size
# build/gwenesis.map` is D/IRAM as a whole (sram_low + sram_high); .text can
# only land in iram0_text, which lives in sram_low alone (see BRINGUP.md
# 0.3-2), so the real spare .text budget is roughly 278KB - 180KB =~ 98KB,
# not 310KB. m68kcpu.c.obj's .text (247KB) cannot fit in that on its own --
# pinning it is the next thing to try, but only after freeing more sram_low
# elsewhere (see the audit report's ranked list for how to measure whether
# that's even worth doing before attempting it).
#
# What's pinned here instead is every *other* gwenesis object's .text --
# VDP line rendering, the Z80 disassembly helper, both sound chips, the
# cartridge I/O glue, savestate glue, and YM2612 -- 30224B total, comfortably
# inside the ~98KB budget. Z80.c itself (57566B, the Z80 CPU interpreter) is
# left out too: added to the safe set it would leave only ~10KB of margin,
# too tight given other things also compete for sram_low as the app grows.
# m68kcpu.c (the 68000 interpreter that actually dominates the frame loop)
# stays in flash for now -- this change alone is not expected to close most
# of the gap to the STM32 port's speed, but it is a real, measurable,
# buildable step in the right direction, and isolates how much of the gap
# these smaller pieces account for versus the 68000 core itself.
[mapping:gwenesis_iram]
archive: libgwenesis.a
entries:
    ym2612 (noflash_text)
    gwenesis_vdp_gfx (noflash_text)
    gwenesis_vdp_mem (noflash_text)
    gwenesis_bus (noflash_text)
    z80inst (noflash_text)
    gwenesis_sn76489 (noflash_text)
    gwenesis_io (noflash_text)
    gwenesis_savestate (noflash_text)
